// A lambda compiled to ONE shared kernel ignores per-call-site catch
// COVERAGE: once an earlier call site of the lambda was uncovered, a
// later call site that IS covered silently drops its error delivery, so
// the handler never runs.
//
//   interp: 0 then 1   (the covered `f(0)` delivers; n becomes 1)
//   jit:    0          (the handler never fires)
//
// `catch` opens an implicit nested scope: statements AFTER it compile
// with the dynamic path extended by `c<spec.id>`, and coverage is
// resolved by path depth (commit 2d24d1b4). So the two `f(i64:0)` call
// sites here have genuinely different coverage — the first is
// uncovered, the second is covered by the catch between them. In the
// node-walk each call site instantiates its own body, so each `?` node
// resolves its own handler. The fused lambda is compiled ONCE and its
// interior `?` carries a single `handler_site`, decided by whichever
// call site drove the kernel build — the uncovered one. The covered
// site then reuses that kernel and its error goes nowhere.
//
// Handler-independent confirmation (rules out `count`'s own firing):
//   let tag = "none";
//   let f = |i: i64| -> i64 (i64:1 /? i)?;
//   let z = f(i64:0);
//   catch(e) tag <- e ~ "caught";
//   let y = f(i64:0);
//   println("tag=[tag]")
// prints `tag=caught` under --no-fusion and `tag=none` with fusion.
//
// Fix: coverage is part of a call site's identity, so it must key the
// kernel. Either (a) include the resolved `handler_site` (or its
// absence) in the kernel identity used by `discover_lambda_calls` /
// `known_fns`, so two call sites with different coverage compile two
// kernels — the same lesson as the #206 / audit-jul2026 name-collision
// fixes, where resolution had to key on more than the name; or (b) pass
// the handler site IN as a hidden kernel parameter (the DynCall
// QopDeliver site is already looked up per dispatch, so the plumbing
// exists) and let the callee deliver to the caller's handler. (a) is
// the smaller change and matches how per-callsite elaboration already
// works elsewhere; (b) avoids duplicating kernels.
{
  let n = i64:0;
  let f = |i: i64| -> i64 (i64:1 /? i)?;
  let z = f(i64:0);
  catch(e) n <- count(e);
  let y = f(i64:0);
  n
}
